WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.210
EKIN KARASAN: My
name is Ekin Karasan,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.810
and I'm a senior in electrical
engineering and computer

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:06.870
science.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.580
So the goal of my project
was to use capnography

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.820
to diagnose between two
cardiorespiratory conditions,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.790
congestive heart failure and
chronic obstructive pulmonary

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:17.820
disease.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.940
So the problem is that there
are a lot of people suffering

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.480
from respiratory conditions.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.750
However, the
diagnostic tests that

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.520
are used to diagnose
these conditions

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.890
require a lot of effort
from the patient,

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.740
and they might be very hard to
comply with if the patient is

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.370
under a lot of distress.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.700
So what we want to do
is replace these tests

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.640
with capnography, which
is effort-independent

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.350
and very easy to comply with.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.600
So capnography measures
the partial pressure

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.300
of carbon dioxide
in exhaled breath

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.400
as the patient is
breathing normally

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.020
and produces a graph
called a capnogram.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.240
And these capnograms
have distinct shapes

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.360
for different
cardiorespiratory conditions.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.790
So the first step is to produce
a mechanistic model that

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.010
is based on the
compartmental models

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.160
of the respiratory
system, that produces

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.250
an expression for the
partial pressure of carbon

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.100
dioxide, or the
capnogram, as a function

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.220
of respiratory parameters.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.820
And these respiratory parameters
are physiologically relevant.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.440
So the first step is to
pre-process our capnogram

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.030
to extract the portions of
the capnogram corresponding

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.550
to exhalations and then
fit our mechanistic model

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.280
to these exhalations to extract
these respiratory parameters

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.840
and use these respiratory
parameters to actually diagnose

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.680
between the two conditions.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.750
So using these estimated
parameters in a training set,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.570
we train a quadratic
discriminator,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.900
and we use this quadratic
discriminator on a test set

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.630
and apply it on a
patient-by-patient basis

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.050
to obtain an accuracy
of around 87.5%,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.420
which was very successful.

